==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|pudenda||that whereof one ought to feel shame}}, substantive use of the neuter plural [[gerundive]] of {{m|la|pudet||it shames}}; in Latin the usage in the plural form (to mean external [[genitalia]]), was far more common than the singular form, as is also the case in English.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{enPR|pyo͝odĕnʹdəm|pyo͞odĕnʹdəm}}, {{IPA|/pjʊˈdɛndəm/|/pjuːˈdɛndəm/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|pudenda}}

# {{lb|en|usually|in the plural}} An external [[genital]] organ in a [[human]]; especially a woman’s [[vulva]].
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A [[shameful]] part of [[something]].

====Usage notes====
* This term appears far more frequently in the plural — as {{m|en|pudenda}} — than in the singular, [[analogous]]ly with {{m|en|genitalia}}, which is rarely encountered in its obscure singular form {{m|en|genitale}}, and with {{m|en|genitals}}, a ''[[plurale tantum]]'' whose supposed singular form {{m|en|genital}} is an adjective in English.

